course: dictionary
lesson: 8315
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. «Robe» is one of them. The words around it are its context, and they tell you which meaning fits. «A judge is an expert in the law who runs a court case fairly and decides the punishment; British judges' wigs and robes are an old tradition.» Here a robe is a long, loose piece of clothing worn for a special occasion.
explain: At home, a robe is something different. It is a bathrobe, or dressing gown, that you put on after a bath or before you get dressed. Look for clues such as bath or getting dressed. Those words tell you which meaning to use.
explain: Robe can also be a verb, but it is formal. To be robed is to be dressed in a robe. You might read that a priest was robed in white before a service.
explain: To check your meaning, put it into the sentence in place of the word. If «robe» means a judge's garment, does the sentence still make sense? If it means a dressing gown, does the bath clue fit? If it does, you have found the right meaning.
fact: A robe is a long, loose piece of clothing that covers the body.
fact: Judges, priests, kings and graduates may wear robes for ceremonies, and in the Bible Joseph had a «robe of many colours».
fact: «Robe» is often an American word for dressing gown, worn at home after a bath.
fact: In «this white fur is called ermine and was once used on kings' robes», robes were rich clothing worn by kings.
fact: Careful: «une robe» in French means a dress, not a dressing gown.
exercise: In «British judges' wigs and robes are an old tradition», what kind of robe is meant? | A dress for a party | A dressing gown worn after a bath | *A long, loose piece of clothing worn for ceremonies | A type of white fur
exercise: Which meaning of robe fits «She put on her robe after her bath»? | A long wig worn in court | *A dressing gown worn after a bath | A church bell | A kind of stoat
exercise: What does robed mean in «The priest was robed in white»? | Covered in snow | Hidden under a hat | Washed clean | *Dressed in a robe
test: In «this white fur is called ermine and was once used on kings' robes», what was ermine used on? | Ships' sails | *Kings' robes | School bags | Church bells
test: Une robe is French. What is it in English, from «une robe is a dress, not a dressing gown»? | A vest | A basket | *A dress | A dressing gown
exam: Which two meanings of robe are shown in these lessons? | A shoe and a sock | *A ceremony garment and a dressing gown | A wig and a bath | A hat and a scarf
